  "fields": {
    "title": "Design Theory",
    "textContent": {
      "nodeType": "document",
      "data": {},
      "content": [
        {
          "nodeType": "heading-3",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "Description",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "paragraph",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "The definition of the term design results in a multi-layered spectrum, which is to be understood under historical, technical, as well as cultural dimensions. Design not only includes solving problems and going through conception and design processes, but also requires a holistic understanding of one's environment.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "2Ok8oU1adMToUZXOSOXFG9",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "[1]",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "superscript"
                    }
                  ],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "paragraph",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "The design theorist Friedrich von Borries defines design as the conscious shaping of objects, spaces, information and relationships, both in physical and digital form.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "1WM3N8tFkABFXRmsLNdEmA",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "[2]",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "superscript"
                    }
                  ],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": " This makes it clear that design denotes a practice shaped by the conception process, which goes far beyond a purely visual finding of form and is not tied to a final product or medium.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "paragraph",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "The understanding of the design concept and profession is not only characterized by the conception process and visual form-finding, but also by the constant new technical developments and the accompanying changes in the general conditions.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
